---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO, loadRelease } from '../lib/site.mjs';

const release = loadRelease();
---
<Base title="Privacy" version={release.version} description="What blockcharts, its skill and this website collect: nothing.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lead">Short version: nothing about you or your data is collected, by the skill, by the reports it makes, or by this website.</p>

    <h2>The skill and the composer</h2>
    <p>
      The skill runs one program, the composer, on your computer or in Claude’s sandbox. It reads the page description and the CSV files
      next to it and writes an HTML file. It makes no network requests and sends your data nowhere. What Claude itself does with a
      conversation is covered by Anthropic’s privacy policy, not this one.
    </p>

    <h2>The reports</h2>
    <p>
      A composed report contains its data and its code and loads nothing from the network: no fonts, no scripts from a CDN, no analytics.
      Opening one tells no one that it was opened.
    </p>

    <h2>The library from a CDN or npm</h2>
    <p>
      If you load blockcharts into your own pages from a CDN, that CDN sees the requests for its files, as with any file it serves. The
      library itself sends nothing anywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      blockcharts.online is a static site. It sets no cookies and runs no analytics or tracking scripts; its fonts and chart code are served
      from the site itself. The one thing it stores is your choice of light or dark theme, in your browser’s local storage, and it never
      leaves your browser. The hosting provider may keep ordinary server logs (such as IP addresses) for security and operations.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>Open an issue in the <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>GitHub repository</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
